# Grammar Review

## Grammar Mistakes (Tense & Agreement)

1. **"this might seem to an unwarranted assault"** — should be "seem to *be* an unwarranted assault" (missing verb).

2. **"My analysis back then was exactly on the same line of logic"** — should be "on the same *lines* of logic" or "along the same lines of logic" (idiomatic error).

3. **"I had no ounce of doubt that they can cut costs"** — tense inconsistency; should be "that they *could* cut costs" to match "had."

4. **"Those who read history knows"** — subject-verb agreement error; should be "Those who read history *know*."

5. **"every industry goes through this phrase"** — should be "this *phase*" (wrong word).

6. **"a reasonable expectation what their careers would look like"** — should be "a reasonable expectation *of what* their careers would look like" (missing preposition).

7. **"there's actually no tech jobs here"** — agreement error; should be "there are actually no tech jobs here" (or "there's actually no tech job market here").

8. **"People in more mature industries generally have a reasonable expectation what their careers would look like"** — tense inconsistency; "would look like" should arguably be "will look like" given the present-tense framing, though this is more debatable.

## Serious Stylistic Issues

1. **"McKinsey Pichai"** — the nickname is used without any introduction or explanation; readers unfamiliar with this reference will be confused. Consider explaining it briefly.

2. **"but In America We Trust they're capitalist enough"** — the embedded slogan is grammatically confusing and reads awkwardly; the sentence structure breaks down here.

3. **"as great/shitty as any random gig"** — the slash construction with a profanity is jarring in an otherwise analytical passage; consider rephrasing.

4. **"Abundance in isolated, privileged pockets isn't True Abundance"** — this aphorism appears abruptly with no context or setup; its connection to the surrounding argument is unclear.

5. **"my technical expertise is... in some funny stack"** — the ellipsis and vague phrasing ("some funny stack") obscures the meaning; unclear what point is being made.

6. **"we're still sane though, just unique circumstances"** — sentence fragment; the parenthetical doesn't parse cleanly.

## Other Problems

1. **"Andreessen Horowitz basically saying"** — Andreessen Horowitz is a firm, not a person; the text later refers to "he's probably onto something," creating a pronoun ambiguity (is it the firm or Marc Andreessen?).

2. **"tech stocks were down last year"** — temporal reference may become confusing/dated for readers depending on when this is read; consider anchoring to specific dates.

3. **"McKinsey Pichai is definitely ready to do whatever pleases the shareholders"** — "definitely" is an overclaim given the hedged tone elsewhere; also the tone shift from analysis to mockery is abrupt.

4. **"the free money is draining away"** — metaphor mixing: money "drains" but "free money" as a concept is usually described as "drying up."

5. **"until the labor supply/demand reaches equilibrium"** — the slash construction is imprecise; "supply/demand" isn't a single noun that "reaches equilibrium" — it's the *ratio* or *market* that reaches equilibrium.

6. **"Saving up for 'FI' isn't an aspiration"** — "FI" (financial independence) is used without definition; not all readers will know this acronym. [end of text]


